summary:
- topic: SPESPA Grievance
  text: Following an Executive Session, the board unanimously voted to deny a grievance
    from the South Portland Educational Support Staff Professionals Association (SPESPA).
- topic: SPHS Athletic Complex Workshop Introduction
  text: The board began a multi-part workshop on the future of the SPHS Athletic Complex,
    with this session focused on the debate between natural grass and synthetic turf,
    hearing from district staff, industry experts, and citizen groups.
- topic: Revised Facility Proposal
  text: Superintendent Metheny presented a preliminary revised proposal for the athletic
    complex that would reduce the amount of synthetic turf by 40% and the total project
    cost by 28% compared to the 2024 plan, featuring one synthetic turf practice field
    instead of three.
- topic: Staff Presentations on Field Conditions and Usage
  text: Rick Peruzzi, the city's athletic facilities manager, detailed the history
    of the 60-year-old field, explaining that overuse, compressed seasons, and maintenance
    limitations have led to its current poor condition. Athletics Director Todd Livingston
    argued for synthetic turf to increase playability, reduce weather-related cancellations,
    and accommodate more teams and sports like field hockey on campus.
- topic: Citizen Group Presentations
  text: Two citizen groups presented opposing views. Sarah Woodbury of Defend Our
    Health argued against artificial turf, citing health risks from toxic chemicals
    like PFAS, environmental pollution from microplastics, and concerns about recyclability.
    Jana Grant, representing parents and coaches, advocated for artificial turf to
    provide safe, reliable, and increased access for student-athletes and the broader
    community.
- topic: Industry Expert Q&A
  text: Representatives from design firm Activitas and turf company Geo Surfaces answered
    board questions on the technical aspects of modern turf systems. They addressed
    maintenance costs, usage hours, recyclability programs, stormwater management,
    and the testing protocols for PFAS and other chemicals to ensure safety.
- topic: Public Comment
  text: Numerous citizens spoke during public comment, with passionate arguments on
    both sides. Opponents of turf reiterated concerns about environmental justice,
    plastic waste, and unknown long-term health effects on children. Proponents emphasized
    the immediate need for durable, high-usage fields to support student athletics,
    ensure safety from poor grass conditions, and provide equitable facilities compared
    to neighboring towns.
